<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Saxe-Hildburghausen</h1><p class="st-meta">1680 CE – 1826 CE · capital Hildburghausen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Ernestine duchy in southern Thuringia whose princess's 1810 wedding in Munich gave rise to the Oktoberfest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-duke-of-saxe-hildburghausen">Ernest</a>, the sixth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-i-duke-of-saxe-gotha">Ernest the Pious</a>, received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildburghausen">Hildburghausen</a> in the 1680 partition of the Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> inheritance. He made the small town his residence and was given full ducal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, later adding Eisfeld and Königsberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes built a palace and attracted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenot</a> refugees to a new town quarter. Extravagance led to bankruptcy, and in 1769 an imperial debt commission took over the finances. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-duke-of-saxe-altenburg">Duke Frederick</a>'s daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therese-of-saxe-hildburghausen">Therese</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Crown Prince Ludwig of Bavaria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in October 1810, and the horse race held in her honour became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oktoberfest">Oktoberfest</a>; the Theresienwiese still bears her name.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-gotha">Saxe-Gotha-Altenburg</a> died out, the Ernestine lands were redistributed in 1826. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-duke-of-saxe-altenburg">Duke Frederick</a> exchanged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildburghausen">Hildburghausen</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>, becoming Duke of Saxe-Altenburg, and Hildburghausen went to Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-saxe-hildburghausen.jpg" alt="Duchy of Saxe-Hildburghausen" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Saxe-Hildburghausen_(until_1815).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1680 CE – 1826 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hildburghausen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ernest, Ernest Frederick I, Frederick</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sachsen-Hildburghausen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1753&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-hildburghausen" aria-label="Duchy of Saxe-Hildburghausen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4925 426 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Saxe-Hildburghausen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5245 682L5283 666M5245 682L5244 667M5245 682L5229 694"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5283 666h0M5244 667h0M5229 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5245 682h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5245 682h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hildburghausen. <a href="/atlas?year=1753&amp;focus=duchy-of-saxe-hildburghausen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
